          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Goneril\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly presents Goneril’s love for Lear as true. The rest of her turn amplifies its supposedly unsurpassable degree, making avowal the dominant force regardless of whether the claim is sincere.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The occurrence declares and magnifies filial love but does not position Goneril as undertaking, renewing, or invoking a commitment or obligation. Her reference to child-and-father love measures the claimed emotion rather than affirming that she is bound to specified duties. Lear’s subsequent grant is a reward for her answer, not uptake of a promise made by this expression.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"As much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"We make thee lady: to thine and Albany’s issue\\nBe this perpetual.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The expression occurs in an extended, formally structured response to Lear’s request. Nothing in the supplied scene presents it as a reflex-like affective discharge beyond the avowal itself.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\\n\\nGONERIL.\\nSir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.84,\"reason\":\"In addition to avowing love, the expression functions as competitive self-presentation in Lear’s public love test. Lear has explicitly linked the daughters’ claims about loving him to the size of his bounty, so Goneril’s superlative declaration also advances her claim to merit the largest grant. This strategic or encomiastic function is not fully represented by T, P, or E.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\\nWhere nature doth with merit challenge.—Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Beyond all manner of so much I love you.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public royal proceeding, Lear announces that he is dividing his kingdom and asks each daughter to state how much she loves him, explicitly connecting the answer to his bounty. Goneril addresses Lear first and delivers an elaborate claim that her filial love exceeds ordinary measures.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"unknown\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal occurrence does not establish an undertaking by Goneril; it is an avowal and competitive claim about the magnitude of her love. Lear responds by granting her territory, which shows practical acceptance and reward of her answer but is not uptake of a promise or obligation created by “I love you.”\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"I recognise the supplied work and scene as William Shakespeare’s King Lear, Act I, Scene I.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied passage establishes the utterance’s public, incentivised setting and avowal force, but it does not by itself settle Goneril’s sincerity, her actual affection, or her prior and subsequent conduct toward Lear.\"}"
        }
